@page "/exchart"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Chart

<DocsPage Product="ExChart" Title="Overview"
          Lead="Line, step and point charts of the data your application shows, drawn as SVG by Blazor. Hand ExChart a definition in your data's terms; it draws each series as what it is, and refuses rather than draws something that only looks right.">

    <DocsSection Title="A first chart">
        <p>
            A <code>ChartDefinition</code> is an axis and its series. Each series has an id, a name, a
            Series Kind and its points; hand the definition to <code>ExChart</code> with a size. Move over the
            plot to read every series at that date, click a legend entry to hide it, and drag across the plot
            to narrow the range. Under MudBlazor, <code>MudChartChrome.Default</code> draws the toolbar and the
            context menu with MudBlazor's controls, and the theme's Primary, Secondary and Tertiary colour the
            first three series.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(FirstChart)" Mud="typeof(FirstChartMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Chart/ChartData.cs"])" CodeOpen="true" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Two ways to make a chart">
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>On its own</strong>, from a definition the page builds, as above.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>From a grid's selection</strong>: the grid's context menu charts the selected cells,
                and the chart keeps following the rows it was made from. See
                <a href="exchart/grid">From the grid</a>.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="What ExChart does, and what it leaves to you">
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Every series says what it is.</strong> A level is joined by a line, a rate over an
                interval is a step, an amount per period is drawn per year. A series without a Series Kind is
                refused, not drawn with a default. See <a href="exchart/series">Series and axes</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>It draws only what it is given.</strong> No spline, no extrapolation, no interpolation:
                a missing value is a gap, never a zero, and a curve between quotes is your analytics', declared
                as such.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>It holds no View State.</strong> The hidden series, the range and the axis come back as
                events, for the page to keep, store or hand back. See
                <a href="exchart/tooltip">Tooltip and Chart Filters</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>It is read aloud.</strong> Each chart has a summary for a screen reader, and "View as
                table" shows the values it draws. Its CSV, SVG and PNG are saved on one click. See
                <a href="exchart/export">Reading and export</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Nothing per point reaches JavaScript.</strong> The plot is SVG elements in components that
                skip their render; a pointer move redraws the tooltip and the guide, not the series.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="WebAssembly, and AOT">
        <p>
            AOT is not required. Measured on WebAssembly, ahead-of-time compilation redraws a chart about 30%
            faster, while hiding a series is no faster, and the application's download grows from 2.9 MB to
            22 MB. A chart redraws within a frame without it; turn it on only when a measurement of your own
            pages says so.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>
</DocsPage>
